      (a)    13 building design requirements, which are applicable to areas
            (excluding residential units) accessible to residents or guests of
              residential units or the public of residential buildings, commercial
             buildings, and buildings with  residential units or commercial
              units –

                 (i)   automatic door at one of the main entrances of a residential
                  building or a building with residential unit;

                 (ii)   higher illumination level at regulated route3 (such as path,
                     staircase, ramp, lobby and corridor) in an indoor area;

                  (iii)  wider regulated route in an indoor area;

               (iv)   slip-resistant floor surfaces at regulated route in an indoor
                    area;

              (v)   wider door on a regulated route (applicable to indoor and
                 outdoor areas);

               (vi)  lower door threshold on a regulated route (applicable to
                  indoor and outdoor areas);

               (vii)  larger accessible lift car;

                (viii) mirror-like finishes for internal surface of accessible lift car;

               (ix)  lower risers and wider treads of indoor main circulation
                     staircase;

              (x)   dual-level handrails of indoor main circulation staircase;

               (xi)   slip-resistant floor surfaces at regulated route in an outdoor
                    area;

               (xii) lower risers and wider treads of outdoor main circulation
                     staircase; and

                (xiii) dual-level handrails of outdoor main circulation staircase;
                and

      (b)     three  building  design  requirements, which  are  applicable  to
              residential units in buildings –

                 (i)   wider main entrance door;

                 (ii)   lower threshold of main entrance door; and

                  (iii)  slip-resistant floor surfaces in bathroom, kitchen and toilet.

                                                          /Performance …

3   It covers the route leading from lot boundary (i.e. access to a public street or a pedestrian way)
   to building entrance and common passageways in the building where residents or guests of
   residential units or the public may or are permitted to have access.
